1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,702
Antarctica. A seal rests on a slab of
floating ice. It is out of the water.

2
00:00:05,702 --> 00:00:07,070
It should be safe.

3
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:14,446
Then the fins appear. Five, six, seven of
them, lined up side by side.

4
00:00:15,146 --> 00:00:20,973
They don't attack the ice. They swim at
it, together, and dive at the last second.

5
00:00:20,973 --> 00:00:24,597
A wave rises behind them and rolls across
the floe.

6
00:00:25,297 --> 00:00:30,672
The seal slides. Once. Twice. The hunters
turn and come again.

7
00:00:31,372 --> 00:00:35,975
This is not instinct. It is a technique.
It has to be learned,

8
00:00:35,975 --> 00:00:40,207
practised and passed on, from one
generation to the next.

9
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:43,700
And it is only one of many hunting
traditions,

10
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:48,071
spread across the oceans of the world, all
practised by a single animal.

11
00:00:48,771 --> 00:00:53,867
What if the most dangerous predator in the
sea is not just a hunter… but a culture?

12
00:00:58,367 --> 00:01:02,815
The orca, or killer whale, is not really a
whale in the way we usually picture one.

13
00:01:02,815 --> 00:01:05,763
It is the largest member of the oceanic
dolphin family.

14
00:01:06,463 --> 00:01:11,028
It can grow to nearly ten metres long and
weigh up to ten tonnes.

15
00:01:11,028 --> 00:01:16,366
Females can live an estimated eighty to
ninety years. Males, fifty to sixty.

16
00:01:17,066 --> 00:01:22,591
Orcas live in every ocean on Earth, from
the polar ice to the tropics.

17
00:01:22,591 --> 00:01:28,904
And almost nothing in the sea is safe from
them: fish, seals, sea lions, sharks,

18
00:01:28,904 --> 00:01:30,325
even other whales.

19
00:01:31,025 --> 00:01:35,785
But here is the strange part. No single
orca eats all of these things.

20
00:01:38,485 --> 00:01:44,123
Each population has its own menu, its own
hunting methods and its own social life.

21
00:01:44,123 --> 00:01:49,004
They share the same body, but they live in
completely different worlds.

22
00:01:49,704 --> 00:01:53,377
Scientists call these groups ecotypes. And
to understand them,

23
00:01:53,377 --> 00:01:57,701
we have to start where orcas have been
studied longer than anywhere else:

24
00:01:57,701 --> 00:01:59,537
the coast of the North Pacific.

25
00:02:00,237 --> 00:02:04,865
Over the next twenty minutes, we will meet
eight of these orca worlds.

26
00:02:04,865 --> 00:02:07,509
Some hunt with waves. Some hunt on land.

27
00:02:07,509 --> 00:02:12,335
One pair has driven the ocean's most
famous shark from its own coastline.

28
00:02:13,035 --> 00:02:17,609
By the end, you may never see the word
"killer" in quite the same way again.

29
00:02:19,109 --> 00:02:21,594
Chapter one: the orcas that never mix.

30
00:02:22,294 --> 00:02:23,200
In the 1970s,

31
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:28,500
a Canadian scientist named Michael Bigg
began studying the orcas off British

32
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:29,127
Columbia,

33
00:02:29,127 --> 00:02:33,590
learning to recognise each whale by the
shape of its dorsal fin.

34
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:39,776
He noticed something odd. In the very same
waters, there were two kinds of orcas.

35
00:02:39,776 --> 00:02:41,198
And they did not mix.

36
00:02:41,898 --> 00:02:46,431
The first kind lived in large, tight
family groups and ate fish,

37
00:02:46,431 --> 00:02:50,397
almost always salmon. Today they are known
as residents.

38
00:02:51,097 --> 00:02:55,766
The second kind roamed in smaller groups
and ignored the salmon entirely.

39
00:02:55,766 --> 00:03:00,435
They hunted seals, sea lions and other
whales. Today they carry his name:

40
00:03:00,435 --> 00:03:01,778
Bigg's killer whales.

41
00:03:02,478 --> 00:03:05,141
Side by side, they look almost identical.

42
00:03:05,141 --> 00:03:10,338
But genetic studies suggest the two lines
split more than three hundred thousand

43
00:03:10,338 --> 00:03:10,988
years ago.

44
00:03:11,688 --> 00:03:16,981
In 2024, a team of researchers proposed
that they are not one species at all,

45
00:03:16,981 --> 00:03:22,204
but two, with two separate scientific
names: Orcinus ater for the residents,

46
00:03:22,204 --> 00:03:24,610
and Orcinus rectipinnus for Bigg's.

47
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:29,577
The scientific community has not fully
agreed. That same year,

48
00:03:29,577 --> 00:03:34,188
the Society for Marine Mammalogy
recognised them as two subspecies,

49
00:03:34,188 --> 00:03:37,698
but stopped short of calling them separate
species.

50
00:03:38,398 --> 00:03:42,348
Either way, the lesson is clear. To an
orca, what you eat,

51
00:03:42,348 --> 00:03:47,593
how you hunt and who you travel with is
not a detail. It defines who you are.

52
00:03:48,293 --> 00:03:51,869
And nowhere is that more obvious than at
the bottom of the world.

53
00:03:53,369 --> 00:03:55,459
Chapter two: the wave makers.

54
00:03:56,159 --> 00:03:58,838
In the waters around the Antarctic
Peninsula,

55
00:03:58,838 --> 00:04:03,721
scientists from the United States'
National Oceanic and Atmospheric
Administration

56
00:04:03,721 --> 00:04:06,817
followed a group they called pack ice
killer whales.

57
00:04:07,517 --> 00:04:10,561
These orcas specialise in seals resting on
floating ice.

58
00:04:10,561 --> 00:04:15,017
And they have solved a problem that should
be impossible for an animal that cannot

59
00:04:15,017 --> 00:04:15,887
leave the water.

60
00:04:16,587 --> 00:04:22,455
First, they find a seal. Several orcas
rise and look at it, heads above the
surface.

61
00:04:22,455 --> 00:04:24,830
Researchers call this spy-hopping.

62
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:31,022
Then they back off, line up, and charge
the floe together, beating their tails.

63
00:04:31,022 --> 00:04:36,722
Just before impact, they dive. The water
they push forward keeps going, as a wave.

64
00:04:37,422 --> 00:04:41,111
If the seal holds on, they do it again.
And again.

65
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:46,276
The researchers saw groups repeat the
attack for half an hour or more.

66
00:04:46,276 --> 00:04:52,400
Every wave costs energy. Every wave has to
be timed together. This is coordination,

67
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:53,212
not chance.

68
00:04:53,912 --> 00:04:58,998
And here is the detail that surprised
everyone. Among the seals resting on the
ice,

69
00:04:58,998 --> 00:05:02,063
Weddell seals made up only around fifteen
percent.

70
00:05:02,063 --> 00:05:06,904
Yet in the kills the team recorded,
fourteen out of fifteen were Weddell
seals.

71
00:05:07,604 --> 00:05:11,808
The orcas were not taking whatever they
found. They were choosing.

72
00:05:11,808 --> 00:05:14,802
Why they prefer Weddell seals is still
unknown.

73
00:05:15,502 --> 00:05:20,478
A wave as a weapon. Teamwork as a tool.
But some orcas take an even bigger risk.

74
00:05:20,478 --> 00:05:22,468
They leave the ocean altogether.

75
00:05:23,968 --> 00:05:26,046
Chapter three: the beach raiders.

76
00:05:26,746 --> 00:05:30,244
Punta Norte, in Argentine Patagonia. Every
year,

77
00:05:30,244 --> 00:05:34,397
sea lion colonies on these pebble beaches
fill with pups.

78
00:05:35,097 --> 00:05:39,867
In March and April, the youngest pups
start to play in the shallows.

79
00:05:39,867 --> 00:05:43,444
They are clumsy, slow, and very close to
the waves.

80
00:05:44,144 --> 00:05:47,418
Out at sea, a tall black fin is waiting.

81
00:05:48,118 --> 00:05:51,587
Then the orca does something no ocean
predator should do.

82
00:05:51,587 --> 00:05:54,813
It rides a wave straight onto the beach,
grabs a pup,

83
00:05:54,813 --> 00:05:57,673
and twists its body to slide back into the
sea.

84
00:05:58,373 --> 00:06:00,011
It is extremely dangerous.

85
00:06:00,011 --> 00:06:03,854
An orca that misjudges the wave can end up
stuck on the sand,

86
00:06:03,854 --> 00:06:06,059
where its own weight could kill it.

87
00:06:06,759 --> 00:06:09,377
This is why the technique has to be
taught.

88
00:06:09,377 --> 00:06:14,187
A local ranger reported that only seven
orcas here were known to hunt this way,

89
00:06:14,187 --> 00:06:17,231
and only five of them taught it to younger
whales.

90
00:06:17,931 --> 00:06:21,294
Young orcas have been seen following an
experienced hunter,

91
00:06:21,294 --> 00:06:26,024
staying close to the beach, sharing the
catch, practising without fully
committing.

92
00:06:26,724 --> 00:06:31,628
That same ranger called it a culture at
risk. If the few teachers disappear,

93
00:06:31,628 --> 00:06:33,887
the knowledge disappears with them.

94
00:06:34,587 --> 00:06:38,977
Small prey, big risk. But some orcas have
gone in the opposite direction.

95
00:06:38,977 --> 00:06:41,924
They hunt the biggest animal that has ever
lived.

96
00:06:43,424 --> 00:06:45,282
Chapter four: hunting a giant.

97
00:06:45,982 --> 00:06:49,267
The blue whale. Around twenty metres long
or more.

98
00:06:49,267 --> 00:06:53,406
The largest animal ever known to have
existed. For a long time,

99
00:06:53,406 --> 00:06:57,348
scientists doubted that anything could
kill a healthy adult.

100
00:06:58,048 --> 00:07:01,791
In March 2019, off the south coast of
Western Australia,

101
00:07:01,791 --> 00:07:06,001
researchers on a boat near a deep-sea
canyon watched it happen.

102
00:07:06,701 --> 00:07:09,435
A dozen or so orcas had found a blue
whale.

103
00:07:09,435 --> 00:07:12,042
They had been chasing it for a long time.

104
00:07:12,742 --> 00:07:17,450
The orcas bit into its body and flanks.
Two of them pushed onto the whale,

105
00:07:17,450 --> 00:07:20,695
forcing it under, until it could no longer
breathe.

106
00:07:21,395 --> 00:07:25,516
Then, more orcas arrived. Around fifty
gathered to share the carcass.

107
00:07:26,216 --> 00:07:29,615
Strangely, the first thing they ate was
the tongue,

108
00:07:29,615 --> 00:07:32,348
probably while the whale was still alive.

109
00:07:32,348 --> 00:07:35,214
Nobody knows why it is their preferred
cut.

110
00:07:35,914 --> 00:07:38,087
It was not a one-off. By 2021,

111
00:07:38,087 --> 00:07:44,027
the same research team had documented
three separate blue whale kills in the
area,

112
00:07:44,027 --> 00:07:47,431
published in the journal Marine Mammal
Science.

113
00:07:48,131 --> 00:07:52,520
One of the researchers said it raised the
bar higher than he had ever imagined.

114
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:55,520
And as blue whale numbers slowly recover
from whaling,

115
00:07:55,520 --> 00:07:57,465
these hunts may become more common.

116
00:07:58,165 --> 00:08:03,041
But the most dramatic orca story of the
last decade is not about size.

117
00:08:03,041 --> 00:08:07,569
It is about two individuals. And a fish
that every swimmer fears.

118
00:08:09,069 --> 00:08:10,776
Chapter five: the shark hunters.

119
00:08:11,476 --> 00:08:16,652
For decades, the coast of South Africa was
famous for one thing: great white sharks.

120
00:08:16,652 --> 00:08:20,288
Tourists came from around the world to see
them from cages.

121
00:08:20,988 --> 00:08:26,427
Then, starting around 2015, dead great
whites began washing up on the beaches.

122
00:08:26,427 --> 00:08:32,006
Each one had the same wound. And each one
was missing the same organ: its liver.

123
00:08:32,706 --> 00:08:37,745
The shark's liver is huge and rich in oil.
For a predator, it is pure energy.

124
00:08:38,445 --> 00:08:40,903
The prime suspects were two male orcas,

125
00:08:40,903 --> 00:08:44,875
easy to recognise because their tall
dorsal fins had collapsed.

126
00:08:44,875 --> 00:08:47,396
One bends to the left, one to the right.

127
00:08:48,096 --> 00:08:52,185
Like the sides of a ship, they were named
Port and Starboard.

128
00:08:52,185 --> 00:08:56,408
They had first been reported in 2009, off
the coast of Namibia,

129
00:08:56,408 --> 00:08:59,961
long before anyone connected them to the
dead sharks.

130
00:09:00,661 --> 00:09:06,106
At least seven dead great whites were
found in 2017 alone. Within a few years,

131
00:09:06,106 --> 00:09:11,691
great white sightings in some of their
favourite bays dropped to almost nothing.

132
00:09:12,391 --> 00:09:17,502
The ocean's most famous predator had been
pushed off its own coastline.

133
00:09:17,502 --> 00:09:23,548
In some places, shark cage diving, a whole
local industry built around great whites,

134
00:09:23,548 --> 00:09:24,628
came to a stop.

135
00:09:25,328 --> 00:09:30,422
Then, in June 2023, researchers saw
something new. Starboard, alone,

136
00:09:30,422 --> 00:09:35,367
caught a young great white and killed it
in less than two minutes.

137
00:09:35,367 --> 00:09:41,585
Then he swam past their boat carrying a
piece of its liver. The next day, a
second,

138
00:09:41,585 --> 00:09:46,080
larger great white washed up nearby, also
without its liver,

139
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:49,302
probably from an earlier attack nobody
saw.

140
00:09:50,002 --> 00:09:55,782
The pair have also killed other shark
species. On a single day in February 2023,

141
00:09:55,782 --> 00:10:00,911
at least seventeen sevengill sharks were
found dead off the same coast,

142
00:10:00,911 --> 00:10:01,995
livers removed.

143
00:10:02,695 --> 00:10:06,860
Scientists still don't know why these two
started hunting sharks,

144
00:10:06,860 --> 00:10:11,090
or whether others will learn from them.
But in the world of orcas,

145
00:10:11,090 --> 00:10:12,564
a new habit can spread.

146
00:10:13,264 --> 00:10:17,144
And the ones who guide what the group
knows, it turns out,

147
00:10:17,144 --> 00:10:18,883
are not the biggest males.

148
00:10:20,383 --> 00:10:22,438
Chapter six: the grandmothers.

149
00:10:23,138 --> 00:10:28,498
Very few animals go through menopause.
Humans do. And, as far as we know,

150
00:10:28,498 --> 00:10:34,226
only five other species do: all of them
toothed whales. Orcas are one of them.

151
00:10:34,926 --> 00:10:38,434
A female orca stops having calves partway
through her life.

152
00:10:38,434 --> 00:10:40,812
But she can live for decades afterwards.

153
00:10:41,512 --> 00:10:44,737
For a long time, that made no evolutionary
sense.

154
00:10:44,737 --> 00:10:47,897
Why live so long if you can no longer
reproduce?

155
00:10:48,597 --> 00:10:53,720
Researchers from the University of Exeter
found part of the answer by studying the

156
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:56,782
resident orcas of the North Pacific over
decades.

157
00:10:57,482 --> 00:10:58,848
When salmon are scarce,

158
00:10:58,848 --> 00:11:03,656
older post-menopausal females are more
likely to lead the group as it travels and

159
00:11:03,656 --> 00:11:04,725
searches for food.

160
00:11:04,725 --> 00:11:08,525
They seem to carry the knowledge of where
the fish can be found,

161
00:11:08,525 --> 00:11:11,612
built up over a lifetime of good years and
bad ones.

162
00:11:12,312 --> 00:11:15,455
In a 2019 study of almost four hundred
young orcas,

163
00:11:15,455 --> 00:11:20,385
calves whose grandmother had died in the
previous two years had a mortality rate

164
00:11:20,385 --> 00:11:24,514
four and a half times higher than calves
with a living grandmother.

165
00:11:25,214 --> 00:11:26,118
And in 2024,

166
00:11:26,118 --> 00:11:32,295
a comparison of toothed whale species
found that females in species with
menopause

167
00:11:32,295 --> 00:11:37,869
live around forty years longer than
females of similar species without it.

168
00:11:38,569 --> 00:11:44,350
The extra years are not wasted. They are
spent leading, protecting and teaching.

169
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:51,758
In a society built on knowledge, the
elders are the library.

170
00:11:51,758 --> 00:11:55,899
And when a member of the family dies,
these whales seem to feel it.

171
00:11:57,399 --> 00:11:59,245
Chapter seven: a mother's grief.

172
00:11:59,945 --> 00:12:05,563
On July twenty-fourth, 2018, off the coast
of British Columbia,

173
00:12:05,563 --> 00:12:11,984
a female resident orca known as Tahlequah,
or J35, gave birth to a calf.

174
00:12:12,684 --> 00:12:15,285
The calf died within about half an hour.

175
00:12:17,985 --> 00:12:21,996
But Tahlequah did not let go. She pushed
the body ahead of her,

176
00:12:21,996 --> 00:12:26,135
balancing it on her head, and carried it
as the family travelled.

177
00:12:26,835 --> 00:12:29,072
Day after day. A week. Two weeks.

178
00:12:29,072 --> 00:12:33,546
Other members of her pod were seen taking
turns carrying the calf.

179
00:12:34,246 --> 00:12:36,840
She carried it for about seventeen days,

180
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:41,315
over roughly one thousand six hundred
kilometres. Then she let it go,

181
00:12:41,315 --> 00:12:42,872
and rejoined her family.

182
00:12:43,572 --> 00:12:48,509
The world watched. And many people asked a
question science is still careful with:

183
00:12:48,509 --> 00:12:49,412
was this grief?

184
00:12:50,112 --> 00:12:54,862
We cannot know what an orca feels. But we
can see what she does.

185
00:12:54,862 --> 00:12:59,908
And at the very end of 2024, she did it
again. A new calf, a female,

186
00:12:59,908 --> 00:13:05,623
died within days, and Tahlequah was seen
carrying her body into the new year.

187
00:13:06,323 --> 00:13:11,710
Her story is also the story of her
population. And that population is in
trouble.

188
00:13:13,210 --> 00:13:15,830
Tahlequah belongs to the Southern
Residents,

189
00:13:15,830 --> 00:13:20,476
a community of orcas that lives around the
waters between Washington State and

190
00:13:20,476 --> 00:13:21,488
British Columbia.

191
00:13:22,188 --> 00:13:25,665
They depend on one kind of fish above all:
Chinook salmon.

192
00:13:25,665 --> 00:13:27,644
And Chinook salmon have declined.

193
00:13:28,344 --> 00:13:33,599
In 1995, there were ninety-eight Southern
Residents. By the end of 2021,

194
00:13:33,599 --> 00:13:35,424
there were seventy-three.

195
00:13:35,424 --> 00:13:40,314
They are listed as endangered in both the
United States and Canada.

196
00:13:41,014 --> 00:13:46,060
Their waters are also contaminated by
pollutants that build up in the bodies of
top

197
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:46,668
predators.

198
00:13:47,368 --> 00:13:52,978
There is some good news. The 2026 census
counted seventy-six Southern Residents,

199
00:13:52,978 --> 00:13:57,747
the second year in a row with a small
increase. And in October 2026,

200
00:13:57,747 --> 00:14:01,954
a new calf was spotted, bringing the count
to seventy-seven.

201
00:14:01,954 --> 00:14:07,003
Researchers and governments have argued
for years over how to help them,

202
00:14:07,003 --> 00:14:10,089
from removing dams to restoring salmon
runs.

203
00:14:10,789 --> 00:14:12,051
Seventy-seven whales.

204
00:14:12,051 --> 00:14:16,678
Small enough that every birth and every
death changes the future of the whole

205
00:14:16,678 --> 00:14:17,279
community.

206
00:14:17,979 --> 00:14:20,958
Meanwhile, on the other side of the
Atlantic,

207
00:14:20,958 --> 00:14:25,990
another tiny orca population has made
headlines for a very different reason.

208
00:14:27,490 --> 00:14:29,371
Chapter eight: the rudder raiders.

209
00:14:30,071 --> 00:14:30,826
Since 2020,

210
00:14:30,826 --> 00:14:36,247
sailors off the coasts of Spain and
Portugal have reported a strange
encounter.

211
00:14:36,247 --> 00:14:41,805
A group of orcas approaches a sailboat,
goes straight for the rudder, and pushes,

212
00:14:41,805 --> 00:14:43,109
bites and spins it.

213
00:14:43,809 --> 00:14:46,818
More than seven hundred interactions have
been logged.

214
00:14:46,818 --> 00:14:48,546
At least eight boats have sunk.

215
00:14:49,246 --> 00:14:54,169
Online, the story became a revenge tale:
orcas fighting back against humans.

216
00:14:54,169 --> 00:14:57,732
Most scientists studying them do not
support that idea.

217
00:14:58,432 --> 00:15:02,919
The Iberian orca population is tiny, fewer
than fifty animals,

218
00:15:02,919 --> 00:15:05,596
and considered critically endangered.

219
00:15:05,596 --> 00:15:10,228
Only around fifteen of them have been
involved in the incidents.

220
00:15:10,928 --> 00:15:13,468
The leading explanation is social
learning.

221
00:15:13,468 --> 00:15:17,131
Young orcas seem to copy the rudder
behaviour from older ones,

222
00:15:17,131 --> 00:15:19,672
and the habit has spread through the
group,

223
00:15:19,672 --> 00:15:22,271
the way a game spreads through a
playground.

224
00:15:22,971 --> 00:15:26,540
It may have started with one bad encounter
with a boat.

225
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:30,627
It may have no deeper meaning at all. But
it shows, once again,

226
00:15:30,627 --> 00:15:34,001
how fast an idea can travel through an
orca society.

227
00:15:34,701 --> 00:15:37,928
And that brings us back to the question at
the start of this film.

228
00:15:39,428 --> 00:15:43,107
Wave makers in Antarctica. Beach hunters
in Patagonia.

229
00:15:43,107 --> 00:15:47,400
Blue whale hunters in Australia. Shark
hunters in South Africa.

230
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:51,897
Salmon specialists in the North Pacific.
Rudder spinners in Spain.

231
00:15:52,597 --> 00:15:55,180
Physically, they are almost the same
animal.

232
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:57,880
What separates them is what they have
learned.

233
00:15:58,580 --> 00:16:02,209
Their hunting methods are passed from one
generation to the next.

234
00:16:02,209 --> 00:16:04,331
Their food preferences are traditions.

235
00:16:04,331 --> 00:16:07,067
Their knowledge is carried by the oldest
females.

236
00:16:07,767 --> 00:16:12,593
That is what many scientists mean when
they say orcas have culture. Not art,

237
00:16:12,593 --> 00:16:14,308
not language as we know it,

238
00:16:14,308 --> 00:16:18,436
but behaviour that is learned socially and
shared within a group.

239
00:16:19,136 --> 00:16:22,824
And culture can be fragile. When a
population shrinks,

240
00:16:22,824 --> 00:16:27,877
it can lose more than individuals. It can
lose a way of life, a technique,

241
00:16:27,877 --> 00:16:30,131
a map of where food can be found.

242
00:16:30,831 --> 00:16:34,494
The Patagonian beach hunters depend on a
handful of teachers.

243
00:16:34,494 --> 00:16:39,359
The Southern Residents depend on salmon,
and on the rivers where salmon are born.

244
00:16:39,359 --> 00:16:44,403
The Iberian orcas number fewer than fifty.
Each of these cultures hangs by a thread,

245
00:16:44,403 --> 00:16:46,145
and each thread is different.

246
00:16:46,845 --> 00:16:50,221
Protecting orcas is not just about saving
a species.

247
00:16:50,221 --> 00:16:53,857
It is about saving many different ways of
being an orca.

248
00:16:55,357 --> 00:16:57,737
We gave them a frightening name: killer
whale.

249
00:16:58,437 --> 00:17:02,105
But watch them long enough and a different
picture appears.

250
00:17:02,105 --> 00:17:07,203
Families that travel together.
Grandmothers who lead. Mothers who will
not let go.

251
00:17:07,903 --> 00:17:13,367
Hunters, yes. Perhaps the most skilled
hunters in the ocean. But also teachers,

252
00:17:13,367 --> 00:17:17,725
students and keepers of traditions passed
down for generations.

253
00:17:18,425 --> 00:17:22,518
Out there, right now, a young orca is
watching its elders,

254
00:17:22,518 --> 00:17:25,623
learning how its family has always
survived.

255
00:17:26,323 --> 00:17:30,812
The question is whether we will leave
enough ocean for those lessons to be
passed

256
00:17:30,812 --> 00:17:30,978
on.

257
00:17:35,678 --> 00:17:38,884
If you enjoyed this film, subscribe to
Naturexplore:

258
00:17:38,884 --> 00:17:42,767
a new documentary every Wednesday. And
tell us in the comments:

259
00:17:42,767 --> 00:17:45,233
which orca tradition surprised you most?

